                                    <h2><span class="shaft"><img id="1505-0" src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/icons/patterns/icon_o.png" width=27 height=27 alt="Omnidirectional" title="Omnidirectional" ></span>DPA 4006A</h2>
                    
                    <h2 class="sub">                    Omnidirectional Condenser Microphone</h2>
                    <p>&#8220;4006A&#8221; is DPA&#8217;s name for a prepackaged modular omnidirectional reference microphone, consisting of two components:</p><ul><li><b>MMC4006 Capsule Head</b> - electret condenser, omnidirectional pattern</li><li><b>MMP-A Head Amplifier</b> - transformerless circuitry</li></ul><p>It is essentially a 3rd-generation version of the B&amp;K 4006, which was a phantom-powered omni with a transformer-coupled output. The DPA 4006 (no suffix) was identical to the B&amp;K 4006. The DPA 4006 TL is the 2nd generation design, and replaces the transformer with electronically balanced output circuitry. The 4006A is a minor revision to the TL; the &#8216;A&#8217; revision introduces modularity (meaning, the capsule can be removed), to better compete with the Schoeps &#8220;Collette&#8221; series microphones. The &#8216;A&#8217; revision also introduces some minor specification improvements, especially regarding the mic&#8217;s dynamic range.</p><p>The head amp is compatible with any of the capsules from DPA&#8217;s &#8220;reference series.&#8221; Likewise, the 4006 capsule head can be used on any of the other head amps in this product line.</p><p>The frequency and polar response of the 4006 can be modified by fitting any of the following &#8220;grids&#8221; onto the end of the capsule:</p><table style="width:80%"><tr><td><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/dpa/DPA_DD0251.jpg" width="100" class="thumb" /></td><td><b>DD0251 Free Field Grid</b><br />Designed for close miking, this grid produces the flattest possible response from the 4051/4052; on-axis, the response graph shows a flat line through 10kHz, then a ~1dB lift from 10kHz&ndash;22kHz. The off-axis response rolls off above 3kHz, with a -3dB point at 10kHz.</td></tr><br /><tr><td><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/dpa/DPA_DD0297.jpg" width="100" class="thumb" /></td><td><b>DD0297 Diffuse Field Grid</b><br />Designed for distance miking, this grid produces a flat response through 15kHz. At close distances, on axis, this grid allows a 6dB boost from 10kHz&ndash;20kHz.</td></tr><br /><tr><td><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/dpa/DPA_DD0254.jpg" width="100" class="thumb" /></td><td><b>DD0254 Close Miking Grid</b><br />Designed for close miking, this grid produces a softer high-frequency roll-off than the DD0251.</td></tr><br /><tr><td><img src="//cdn.recordinghacks.com/images/mic_extras/dpa/DPA_UA0777.jpg" width="100" class="thumb" /></td><td><b>UA0777 Nose Cone</b><br />Designed to affect the mic&#8217;s polar pattern rather than its frequency response, the UA0777 counteracts the capsule&#8217;s tendency to attenuate high frequency off-axis sounds. The result is &#8220;an even tonal balance of sound arriving at all angles of incidence,&#8221; albeit with a sharp 5dB boost for on-axis sounds at 12kHz, even in the diffuse field.</td></tr></table><p></p><p>The mic&#8217;s high sensitivity (40mV/Pa) and low self-noise (15dBA) make it ideally suited to close miking of quiet acoustic instruments, as well as for ambient miking of performances.</p><p>The 4006A ships with the three grids pictured above. The UA0777 nosecone is available separately, as are the &#8220;APEs&#8221; (acoustic passive equalizer balls measuring 30mm, 40mm, or 50mm in diameter).<p>The <a href="//recordinghacks.com/microphones/DPA/4006A">DPA 4006A</a> is also known as: 4006.</p>
